    `o 19 r 7(3) the parties are allowed to make an oral submission at the hearing of the appeal` `the oral submission is one the appellate Court orders`

@export Under Order 19, rule 7(4), may the appellate Court make an order on a part of the decision that is not the subject of any appeal?
GIVEN `the order relates to a part of the decision of the lower Court` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the order relate to a part of the lower Court's decision?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under Order 19, rule 7(4), may the appellate Court make an order on a part of the decision that is not the subject of any appeal` IS
    `o 19 r 7(4) the appellate Court may make an order although that part is not the subject of any appeal and the reason is not stated by anyone` `the order relates to a part of the decision of the lower Court`

@export Under Order 19, rule 7(5), is the appellate Court's power to decide the merits restricted because there was no appeal against an earlier order?
GIVEN `the only reason is that there was no appeal against a previous order of the lower Court` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the only reason for the restriction that no one appealed against an earlier order of the lower Court?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under Order 19, rule 7(5), is the appellate Court's power to decide the merits restricted because there was no appeal against an earlier order` IS
    `o 19 r 7(5) the appellate Court's powers to decide the merits are restricted` `the only reason is that there was no appeal against a previous order of the lower Court`

@export Under Order 19, rule 7(6), may the appellate Court order a new trial?
GIVEN `substantial injustice will be caused otherwise` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Will substantial injustice be caused if there is no new trial?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under Order 19, rule 7(6), may the appellate Court order a new trial` IS
    `o 19 r 7(6) the appellate Court may order a new trial` `substantial injustice will be caused otherwise`

@export Under Order 19, rule 7(7), does the appellate Court have power to receive further evidence?
GIVEN `a written law restricts the power to receive further evidence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does a written law restrict the appellate Court's power to receive further evidence?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Under Order 19, rule 7(7), does the appellate Court have power to receive further evidence` IS
    `o 19 r 7(7) the appellate Court has power to receive further evidence` `a written law restricts the power to receive further evidence`

@export Under Order 19, rule 7(7), may further evidence be given on the appeal?
